#import "common.typ": *

In this chapter, we introduce the theoretical foundations and practical tools relevant to the dissertation. We begin by presenting the core concepts in polyhedral analysis, and then demonstrate how they are applied in the context of cache modelling. Subsequently, we discuss relevant aspects of software engineering, including the tools used, requirements analysis, and the starting point of the project.

== Polyhedral Analysis

Polyhedral analysis is focused on _affine loop nests_. In these loops, the loop variable, called an _induction variable_, iterates from a lower bound to an upper bound, and increments by a constant at each step. A typical affine loop is of the form:

```python
for i in lower..upper, step c
  ...
```

In polyhedral analysis, the loop bounds must be _affine expressions_ of the induction variables of their enclosing loops. An affine expression of variables $x_1, x_2, ...$ is their linear combination plus a constant term, namely $a_1x_1 + a_2x_2 + ... + c$, where $a_i$ are compile-time constants.

Loop iterations are uniquely identified by the values of the induction variables of their surrounding loops. These values form a vector, referred to as the _induction vector_, ordered from the outermost to the innermost loop. The range of the induction vector is called the _iteration domain_, denoted by $cal(D)$.

As an example, consider the following loop nest:

```python
for i in 0..10:
  for j in 0..i:
    a[i][j] *= 2   # S
```

The induction vector for statement $S$ can be expressed as $bf(x) = (i, j)$, and its iteration domain is $D = { (i, j) | 0 <= i <= 10, 0 <= j <= i }$.

Across different iterations, the statement $S$ accesses different memory addresses. To distinguish them, we denote by $S(bf(x))$ the instance of $S$ executed on iteration $bf(x) = (i, j)$, and by $S[bf(x)]$ the address that $S$ accesses on iteration $bf(x)$. In the example above, $S[bf(x)] = \&a[i][j]$.

=== Computing Dependency <dep-compute>

When considering cache behaviour, we consider statement $S(bf(x))$ to depend on a previous statement $T(bf(y))$ if and only if they access the same address #footnote[To be more precise, it suffices that $S(bf(x))$ and $T(bf(y))$ access the same cache line. An extension of the project takes this into account, as described in @cacheline-size.]. Modern caches are typically write-back, so we do not care whether $S$ and $T$ are reads or writes, since either operation brings the accessed data into the cache.

Formally, $S(bf(x))$ depends on $T(bf(y))$ when the following conditions hold:

+ $bf(x)$ and $bf(y)$ are within iteration domains $cal(D)_S$ and $cal(D)_T$ respectively;

+ $S[bf(x)] = T[bf(y)]$;

+ $T(bf(y))$ is executed before $S(bf(x))$.

When $S$ and $T$ are in the same loop nest, the third condition translates to $bf(x) lexge bf(y)$, where $lexge$ denotes the lexicographically larger relation. When $S$ and $T$ share some but not all enclosing loops, however, it is no longer equivalent to $bf(x) lexge bf(y)$. The complicated case is explained in detail in @comp-miss-count.

The definition relates $S(bf(x))$ to every $T(bf(y))$ that accesses the same address, but for our use cases, we need only the latest one. This instance is the lexicographically greatest $T(bf(y))$, denoted as $deps(S(bf(x)))$.

It is possible that there is no $T(bf(y))$ that $S(bf(x))$ depends on. In this case, we consider $deps(S(bf(x)))$ as undefined.

#include "../diagrams/dep-example.typ"

As an example, in @dep-example, the statement $S(j)$ depends on statement $T(i)$ when and only when $j = i$, so $deps(S(j)) = T(j)$. Meanwhile, $T(i)$ does not depend on any other statement, so $deps(T(i))$ is undefined everywhere.

=== Reuse Distance

The reuse distance of $S(bf(x))$, denoted $dist(S(bf(x)))$, is the number of unique addresses accessed by statements executed between $deps(S(bf(x)))$ and $S(bf(x))$. In other words, this is the number of cache lines evicted between $S[bf(x)]$ and its last usage, if we assume each cache line holds a single element.

#include "../diagrams/dist-example.typ"

Consider the program in @dep-example. The value of $dist(S(6))$ is $9$, and $dist(S(5))$ is $10$. @dist-example graphically depicts the situation, where orange blocks denote the load $S(j)$ and its dependency $T(j)$, and blue blocks stand for the cache lines accessed between them. Note that when $j = 5$, the address `a[6]` is accessed twice, but it counts only once since we are concerned with _unique_ addresses.

=== Counting Cache Misses <cache-modelling>

Following the setting of our reference cache model, Falcon, we assume that the cache is fully associative, uses LRU replacement, and that each array element takes up an entire cache line. We denote the cache size by $C$, and consider each statement $S$ individually.

The number of compulsory misses generated by $S$ is equal to the count of induction vectors $bf(x)$ where $deps(S(bf(x)))$ is undefined.

The number of capacity misses caused by $S$ is given by the count of $bf(x)$ such that $dist(S(bf(x))) >= C$, meaning that every cache line has been evicted since the last access of $S[bf(x)]$.

To efficiently count $bf(x)$ under these constraints, we utilise Barvinok's algorithm, which we introduce in the following section.

== Barvinok's Algorithm <barvinok>

Barvinok's algorithm @Barvinok counts the integer points inside a _polyhedron_.

=== Polyhedra

An $n$-dimensional polyhedron $P$ can be viewed as a set of inequalities in $n$ variables, each of which defines half of the entire space $bb(R)^n$. The polyhedron is the intersection of all these halfspaces. This representation is called the _H-representation_, where 'H' stands for halfspace.

Formally, the H-representation of a polyhedron $P$ is defined by a matrix $A$ and a vector $bf(b)$, such that $P = { bf(x) in bb(R)^n | A bf(x) + bf(b) >= 0 }$. Unless otherwise stated, we only consider polyhedra where every element of $A$, $bf(b)$ and $bf(x)$ is an integer.

#include "../diagrams/halfspace.typ"

For example, consider a triangle shown in @figure-halfspace, which is a 2-dimensional polyhedron. It can be expressed with 3 inequalities:
$
  3x - y &>= 0 \
  x - 4y &>= 0 \
  2x + 3y - 11 &>= 0
$

The coloured parts are halfspaces indicated by each inequality.

At every vertex $v$ of a $n$-dimensional polyhedron, at least $n$ inequalities will become equalities. These inequalities are said to be _active_ at $v$. In the previous example, the first two inequalities are active at vertex $(0, 0)$.

For a polyhedron $P$ and its vertex $v$, the active inequalities at $v$ form another polyhedron, which is in fact a cone. This cone is called the _tangent cone_ at $v$.

=== Cones <cones>

Cones are special cases of polyhedra. They consist of rays cast from the origin.

#include "../diagrams/cone-example.typ"

Formally, a cone $C$ can be described by a set of vectors ${ bf(v)_i }$, where $C = { sum_i lambda_i bf(v)_i | lambda_i >= 0 }$. The vectors $bf(v)_i$ are called _generators_. This description method is called the _V-representation_ of a cone.

The generators of $C$ can form a matrix, with each of them taking up a column:

#set math.mat(delim: "[")
$ V = mat(bf(v)_1, bf(v)_2, dots.h, bf(v)_n) $

The cone $C$ is said to be _unimodular_ if $V$ is a square matrix, and the determinant of $V$ is $plus.minus 1$.

The cone $dual(C) = { bf(x) | V bf(x) >= 0 }$ in H-representation is called the _dual_ of $C$.

=== Algorithm Overview <barvinok-alg-overview>

@barvinok-overview shows the overall structure of Barvinok's algorithm. The same steps have to be repeated for all other tangent cones to obtain the integer count, $10$, for the polyhedron shown in the figure.

In the figure, green arrows indicate the steps we implemented in FPL. Red arrows indicate bugs that we fixed in the current implementation, and black arrows indicate functionality that was already complete and that we use without modification.

The algorithm works only on full-dimensional polyhedra. For a lower-dimensional polyhedron $P$, a preprocessing step is needed to project it to a full-dimensional polyhedron $Q$ with the same number of integer points.

#include "../diagrams/barvinok-overview.typ"

Barvinok's algorithm first identifies the tangent cone $C$ at each vertex $bf(u) = (u_1, dots, u_n)$ of $Q$. Then it decomposes the dual cone $dual(C)$ into a signed sum of unimodular cones $U_i$. @barvinok-overview decomposes the original cone $C$ instead, which is also valid, but in that case lower-dimensional cones resulting from the decomposition must also be considered @LaTTE.

For each $n$-dimensional unimodular cone $U_i$ with $m$ generators $bf(v)_1, dots, bf(v)_m$, the algorithm then derives a rational function called the _generating function_ with $n$ variables $x_1, dots, x_n$. The function is of the form

$
  x_i^(u_i) / (product_(j=1)^m (1 - product_(i=1)^n x_i^bf(v)_(j i)))
$

Finally, the algorithm evaluates the limit of the function at $bf(x) = 1$, and the results are summed up to obtain the integer count.

== The Fast Presburger Library <fpl>

We introduce the mathematics library used in the project, the Fast Presburger Library @FPL, abbreviated as FPL.

FPL operates on _integer relations_, formed by conjunctions of equalities and inequalities in _presburger arithmetic_, that is, affine expressions of integer variables. Equivalently, an integer relation can also be viewed as the H-representation of an integer polyhedron of the form ${ bf(x) | A bf(x) + bf(b) = 0, C bf(x) + bf(d) >= 0 }$.

FPL also supports _presburger relations_, which are finite unions of integer relations. Each component of such a union is referred to as a _disjunct_.

FPL provides the following primitives:
- union and intersection;
- subtraction;
- emptiness test.

The library also offers various auxiliary mathematical utilities, such as normal form computation on integer matrices. We will highlight these FPL procedures used in our implementation in later sections.

The operations on integer relations are implemented based on a variant of the Simplex algorithm @Simplify. By exposing the operations on Simplex objects, FPL also provides functionality for integer linear programming. In particular, FPL is capable of computing the minimum and maximum of an affine expression $bf(k) dot bf(x) + b$ where $bf(x)$ belongs to an integer relation $P$.

=== Variable Types

FPL distinguishes four types of variables:
- *Domain* variables, representing inputs of the relation;
- *Range* variables, representing outputs of the relation;
- *Symbol* variables, representing unknown but fixed symbolic constants;
- *Local* variables, which are existentially quantified.

This distinction motivates treating integer relations as *relations* rather than *polyhedra* in the library. The design enables clearer semantic interpretation and facilitates relation composition, which is frequently used in our cache analysis pass.

As an example, given a symbolic constant $N$, the function $y = floor(x / 2) + N$ can be expressed as the following relation:

$
  { (x, y) in bb(Z)^2 | exists e in bb(Z). y = e + N, x <= 2e <= x + 1 }
$

In this relation, $x$ is a domain variable, $y$ is a range variable, $N$ is a symbol variable, and $e$ is a local variable. The example also demonstrates how division by an integer constant is represented via introduction of extra local variables.

For presburger relations, the local variables are _local_ to each of its disjuncts. One disjunct is unable to access local variables defined in another disjunct.

=== Diversion from Original Design

The original design of FPL @FPL employs a transprecision strategy. Computation begins in 16-bit integers, and promotes to 64-bit and then arbitrary-precision integers when an overflow is detected. This approach is more cache-friendly and enables the use of SIMD instructions in common cases.

However, the upstream implementation of FPL inside MLIR does not include the transprecision feature. In the library, every integer is represented with an arbitrary-precision integer type `DynamicAPInt`. This design choice is motivated by two reasons:

- The precision switch relies on C++ exceptions, which are disabled in the LLVM project.

- SIMD intrinsics are not suitable for a portable, cross-platform codebase.

As a result, the MLIR version of FPL is less optimised and can perform worse than _isl_. However, as the library is tightly integrated into MLIR, it remains a desirable choice for our project.

== Clang IR

The project is implemented on top of MLIR (_Multi-Level Intermediate Representation_), a compiler infrastructure framework that supports custom definition of different forms of intermediate representations (IRs). These IRs are called _dialects_.

We focus on Clang IR, an MLIR dialect designed for the `clang` C++ compiler. The dialect is commonly abbreviated as `cir`. The Clang IR project also provides a frontend that converts an abstract syntax tree (AST) from `clang` into an MLIR module in `cir`.

=== Structural Characteristics

Clang IR is in static single assignment (SSA) form and has structured control flow.

As opposed to LLVM IR, which represents control flow with branches between basic blocks and relies on analyses to recover high-level constructs, Clang IR encodes the constructs explicitly with operations that carry _regions_. For example, a while-loop is typically of the form

```mlir
cir.while %cond {
  loop body
}
```

A region is a container of basic blocks, and a basic block contains a list of operations. An operation can also own nested regions. This mutually recursive design forms a tree structure in Clang IR, whose root is a ```mlir cir.module``` operation.

The explicit control flow operations simplify our analysis, which heavily depends on loop structure, as we no longer need dominance analysis to recover it.

=== $phi$ nodes

In SSA, $phi$ nodes are used to merge values at a control flow join point, selecting the value offered by the predecessor block that was executed. They are commonly placed at the beginning of the merge-point block as pseudo-instructions.

In most dialects in MLIR, the result of a $phi$ node is instead encoded as _block arguments_, and the operands of a $phi$ node are viewed as operands of the branch instruction that terminates each predecessor block.

However, Clang IR currently does not utilise either form of $phi$ nodes. Reads and writes to local variables are translated into loads and stores at a stack address. As a result, value merging is represented implicitly via memory state, rather than explicitly in SSA.

This situation makes it harder to identify the induction variable of a loop, and creates the need for preprocessing passes on Clang IR, one of which analyses common for-loop patterns. We discuss IR patterns in more detail in @ir-raising.

== Requirements Analysis <req-analysis>

According to the proposal, the project has a single deliverable: a static analysis pass that estimates cache misses for affine programs using a polyhedral model.

=== Problem Scope

Our analysis is restricted to _affine programs_, following the standard definition in polyhedral analysis:

- Loop bounds are known at compile time;

- Array indices are affine expressions of induction variables;

- Control flow depends only on induction variables, and never on runtime data.

These restrictions ensure that the program can be represented in a polyhedral model.

=== Implementation Strategy

Based on the cache-modelling algorithm described in @cache-modelling, the project can be separated into two parts: implementing the polyhedral cache model and completing Barvinok's algorithm.

Although Barvinok's algorithm is conceptually independent of the cache model, it relies on our cache model to generate test cases in order to verify its correctness and check coverage of corner cases. Following this reasoning, the two components are developed in an interleaved manner, with priority given to the cache model.

To guide development, we construct a dependency graph as in @dependency-graph, where nodes in blue correspond to stages of the model, and nodes in green correspond to components of Barvinok's algorithm. @barvinok-impl provides a detailed explanation of each component.

#include "../diagrams/dependency-graph.typ"

Our development progresses by repeatedly selecting nodes without unmet requirements, similar to a topological ordering of the graph. Cache model stages are prioritised as they provide test cases for the counting algorithm.

=== Validation Strategy

Correctness is checked incrementally. After a cache model stage is implemented, the partially complete analysis is executed on 6 small test programs. The intermediate results are then compared with the expected results obtained either manually or from a cache simulator.

After the implementation is finished, the system is evaluated on another 28 programs to test its correctness, accuracy and efficiency. Details of the evaluation are provided in @eval.

=== Libraries and Tools <lib-tools>

Debugging and testing rely heavily on LLVM infrastructure. In particular, the assertion facilities in LLVM are extensively used to validate preconditions and postconditions of mathematical procedures. Unit tests and regression tests for Barvinok's algorithm and existing methods in FPL are conducted using `llvm-lit`, a testing tool within LLVM.

The GNU debugging tool `gdb` is used only sparingly due to the high memory and time costs of compiling LLVM with debug information.

Static analysis tools such as `clang-tidy` and `clangd` are employed to lint the code and maintain code quality. Versions are managed with `git`, and the repository is periodically backed up to our Clang IR fork on GitHub.

== Starting Point

Prior to the start of the project, I was already familiar with C++, compiler internals, the MLIR framework, and the Clang IR repository. My experience with Clang IR during the Part IB group project was greatly helpful. Over the summer, I read about the mathematics involved in polyhedral analysis, and learnt Barvinok's algorithm.

The project is based on Clang IR git commit `e4003cd3a263`. The commit was merged on Nov 10, 2025, but I did not write any code for this project before October 2026. No further pulls from the main branch were performed during the course of the project.
